  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">GIZ-SHADE Gender Responsive Employable Skills Development</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Cox&apos;s Bazar is one of the poorest districts in Bangladesh (BBS 2017) with the local economy heavily dependent on traditional industries like fishing and agriculture. Lack of infrastructural development hindered industrialization impacting economic progress (World Bank, 2020). The arrival of Forcibly Displaced Myanmar Nationals (FDMNs) in 2017 severely impacted the local economy and livelihoods of the host community, and increased living costs as competition for jobs, and resources increased (World Bank, 2019). Along with the economic challenges, women in the host communities also face challenges related to insecurity, limited mobility, lack of income opportunities, increased domestic violence, marital instability, polygamy, sexual harassment, exploitation, and discrimination by service providers due to decreased access to services (GIHA WG, 2022). These challenges affect the well-being, dignity, and rights of women in Cox&apos;s Bazar host community.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The objective of the project is to contribute to peaceful conflict resolution, addressing the root causes of conflicts within host communities and FDMN displaced persons through Gender-responsive and inclusive approaches.

The project will develop skills, employment opportunities, women-friendly &amp; inclusive spaces for the FDMNs and build the capacities of local state and non-state actors and FDMN community people for preventing and dealing with GBV.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Total number of beneficiaries under point 1 is 750 FDMN, out of them at least 375 women and at least 50 persons with disabilities and/or other minority groups.
Total number of beneficiaries under point 3.2 is 250 state and non-state actors including 150 women and at least 10 persons with disabilities. 
Total number of beneficiaries under point 3.3 is 250 FDMN community members including 150 women and at least 15 persons with disabilities.</narrative>
  </description>
